turns.reply:
The bug is that the `samesign` flag on an `icmp` instruction makes the comparison potentially poison if the operand sign bits differ. The transformation of a `select` into an `xor` (or similar) speculatively executes the `icmp` instructions, which introduces poison if the `samesign` flag is present and the sign bits happen to differ, even if the original `select` was well-defined (because it only executed the `icmp` when safe).

`isKnownInversion` is used to prove that two `icmp` predicates are inverses, allowing `InstCombine` to perform the unsafe transformation. By making `isKnownInversion` return `false` when `samesign` is present, we prevent the unsafe transformation. While this causes existing tests that expected this transformation to fail, this is necessary to prevent the miscompilation.

