The issue is that `foldPHIArgGEPIntoPHI` blindly attempts to merge GEPs into a single GEP of a PHI, even when one of the GEPs depends on the PHI being folded. This creates a cycle where the new PHI depends on the old PHI, and the old PHI depends on the new GEP (which depends on the new PHI). While this can be valid for PHI nodes in loops, the current implementation can lead to miscompilations when the transformation is applied in contexts where it creates invalid IR or triggers undefined behavior (like poison propagation).

The transformation is fundamentally unsafe when the GEPs being merged depend on the PHI itself, as it creates a circular dependency that `InstCombine` does not correctly handle in this PHI-folding context. We must restrict the transformation to cases where the GEPs do not use the PHI being folded.

This restriction is safe and correct, and the failure in `gepphigep.ll` occurred because I previously added an overly aggressive check that prevented valid transformations. By refining the check to ensure we only reject cases where the GEP *directly* uses the PHI being folded, we resolve the issue while preserving the optimization for cases where it is safe.

